# A Chemically Stable HofmannâType MetalâOrganic Framework with SandwichâLike Binding Sites for Benchmark Acetylene Capture

## abstract
The realization of porous materials for highly selective separation of acetylene (C2 H2 ) from various other gases (e.g., carbon dioxide and ethylene) by adsorption is of prime importance but challenging in the petrochemical industry. Herein, a chemically stable Hofmann-type metal-organic framework (MOF), Co(pyz)[Ni(CN)4 ] (termed as ZJU-74a), that features sandwich-like binding sites for benchmark C2 H2 capture and separation is reported. Gas sorption isotherms reveal that ZJU-74a exhibits by far the record C2 H2 capture capacity (49 cm3 g-1 at 0.01 bar and 296 K) and thus ultrahigh selectivity for C2 H2 /CO2 (36.5), C2 H2 /C2 H4 (24.2), and C2 H2 /CH4 (1312.9) separation at ambient conditions, respectively, of which the C2 H2 /CO2 selectivity is the highest among all the robust MOFs reported so far. Theoretical calculations indicate that the oppositely adjacent nickel(II) centers together with cyanide groups from different layers in ZJU-74a can construct a sandwich-type adsorption site to offer dually strong and cooperative interactions for the C2 H2 molecule, thus leading to its ultrahigh C2 H2 capture capacity and selectivities. The exceptional separation performance of ZJU-74a is confirmed by both simulated and experimental breakthrough curves for 50/50 (v/v) C2 H2 /CO2 , 1/99 C2 H2 /C2 H4 , and 50/50 C2 H2 /CH4 mixtures under ambient conditions.

A Chemically Stable Hofmann-Type Metal−Organic Framework with Sandwich-Like Binding Sites for Benchmark Acetylene Capture
The realization of porous materials for highly selective separation of acetylene $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ from various other gases (e.g., carbon dioxide and ethylene) by adsorption is of prime importance but challenging in the petrochemical industry. Herein, a chemically stable Hofmann-type metal−organic framework (MOF), $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{p y z})[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]$ (termed as ZJU-74a), that features sandwich-like binding sites for benchmark ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ capture and separation is reported. Gas sorption isotherms reveal that ZJU-74a exhibits by far the record ${\sf C}_{2}{\sf H}_{2}$ capture capacity $(49c m^{3}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.01 bar and 296 K) and thus ultrahigh selectivity for $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ (36.5), $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (24.2), and $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (1312.9) separation at ambient conditions, respectively, of which the $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ selectivity is the highest among all the robust MOFs reported so far. Theoretical calculations indicate that the oppositely adjacent nickel(II) centers together with cyanide groups from different layers in ZJU-74a can construct a sandwich-type adsorption site to offer dually strong and cooperative interactions for the ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ molecule, thus leading to its ultrahigh $C_{2}H_{2}$ capture capacity and selectivities. The exceptional separation performance of ZJU-74a is confirmed by both simulated and experimental breakthrough curves for 50/50 (v/v) ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2},$ $\mathbb{1}/99\mathbb{C}_{2}\mathbb{H}_{2}/\mathbb{C}_{2}\mathbb{H}_{4},$ and $50/50\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ mixtures under ambient conditions.
Acetylene $\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ is an important industrial gas that has been widely used as fuels in welding/cutting or as feedstocks for the production of commercial chemicals such as vinyl ­chloride, acrylic acid, and 1,4-butynediol.[1,2] $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is typically ­generated by the combustion of natural gas or the cracking of hydrocarbons, inevitably coexisting with some of carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ or unconverted methane $\mathrm{(CH}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ .[3] Purification of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is of industrial importance to obtain highpurity $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for the manufacture of relative chemicals. Further, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is unstable and highly reactive that may induce some undesirable reactions in various industrial processes.[4] For instance, a trace amount of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ acts as a catalyst poison during ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ polymerization processes, which needs to be removed to an acceptable level of no more than $40~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Therefore, acetylene capture and separation is of industrial relevance to two different points of view: the removal of trace $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ impurities from ethylene, and the purification of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from contaminants for its pure form. Compared with traditional solvent extraction or cryogenic distillation,[5,6] adsorptive separation based on porous materials is more environmentally friendly and energy-efficient for separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from other gases. In this context, realization of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -selective adsorbents with strong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture capacity is highly desirable to target high performance for all these gas separations.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers have emerged as promising adsorbents for gas separation and purification owing to their powerful predictability and tunability on pore size/shape and functionality.[7–9] Since $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (kinetic diameter: $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) has a relatively large size or polarity difference with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (4.2 Å) and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (3.8 Å), highly efficient separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ or $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ has been fulfilled in some reported MOFs with benchmark selectivities by pore tuning and pore functionalization strategies.[10,11] However, compared with the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation is much more difficult and challenging because of their very close molecular size/shape $(3.3\ \mathring\mathrm{\A}$ for both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ and physical properties.[12] Fine tuning of pore size or flexibility on MOFs is quite limited to achieve high separation performance for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation.[13] In this context, the most popular strategy is to immobilize functional groups/sites onto the pores to enforce the binding affinity with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and thus enhance $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity.[14–20]
The well-established cases of MOFs are those with open metal sites (OMSs) that can bind acetylene significantly. For example, MOF-74 series shows high density of OMSs that have strong interactions with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , leading to their high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes and moderate $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivities.[17,18] The isomer UTSA-74 has a higher volumetric density of OMSs $(8.25\mathrm{\mmol\cm^{-3}}.$ than MOF-74 $(7.50\mathrm{\mmol\cm^{-3}},$ ), thus exhibiting an enhanced $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity.[19] Further increase on the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity in NKMOF-1-Ni with OMSs and additional binding sites affords the benchmark $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity of 22 at ambient conditions.[20] Evidently, the design of porous materials with strong binding affinity for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ provides a promising strategy to address industrial challenges related to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation, especially for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation.
Current MOFs with OMSs typically allow one adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule to interact with one metal site due to inadequate overlap of potential fields between two metal sites. Such single metal– ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction would delimit the acetylene binding strength and thus the capture capacity at low-pressure region in most cases, as exemplified by HKUST-1,[17b] $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF-74,[17a] and UTSA-74.[19] To achieve stronger $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding within a MOF, we speculated that if two opposite coordinatively unsaturated metal centers are adjacent to each other with a suitable distance, a sandwich-like binding model for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can be generated based on the dual and cooperative interactions between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule and the two metal sites, thus probably affording ultrahigh $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding affinity and capture capacity. With this in mind, we herein realized a chemically stable Hofmann-type ultramicroporous MOF, $\mathrm{Co(pyz)[Ni(CN)_{4}]}$ (ZJU-74, $\mathrm{\pyz=\pyra\cdot}$ zine) that features oppositely adjacent open metal ions and densely populated cyanide groups thus providing a sandwichlike binding environment to capture $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules (Figure 1). Our experimental and simulation studies verify this hypothesis, and the activated ZJU-74a shows the benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity and thus the highest $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $49\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.01 bar and $296\ \mathrm{K},$ as well as very high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivities under such conditions.
Complete structures for ZJU-74 have not been determined, and the likely model was simulated based on powder diffraction data in the previous literatures reported.[21] To get the accurate structure information, orange square-block-shaped crystals of ZJU-74 suitable for X-ray analysis were prepared by slow diffusion of a methanol/water solution of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and pyz into a ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}]\cdot\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution for 2 weeks. The singlecrystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that ZJU-74 adopts a 3D network that crystallizes in a tetragonal $P4/\mathrm{mmm}$ space group. As shown in Figure 1a, four terminal nitrogen atoms of the $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ anion units are coordinated to cobalt(II) atoms to generate a 2D square grid-like $\{{\mathrm{Co}}[{\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{4}]\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ layers. The 2D layers are pillared by the pyz ligands to form a 3D network without interpenetration. Thus, each ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ site shows six coordinated octahedral geometry, completed by six nitrogen atoms originated from four $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions and two pyz ligands. Topologically, ZJU-74 possesses a 4,6-connected $f s c$ -net. This network yields channels with dimensions of about $3.8\times3.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $a$ or $b$ axis. Due to the planarity of $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ units, each Ni(II) site shows two accessible binding sites perpendicular to the layer plane. The calculated density of open Ni(II) sites can reach $9.05~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which is even higher than the well-known MOF-74 (7.50 mmol $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ) and UTSA-74 $(8.25\mathrm{\mmol\cm^{-3}})$ . In addition, due to the small pore size of ZJU-74, the Ni-Ni distance from adjacent layers are only $3.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ after subtracting the van der Waals radius, which is very suitable to trap a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule $(3.3\mathring\mathrm{\A})$ . As a result, these adjacent two $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ building units along with a suitable pore size can form a sandwich-like binding environment to efficiently capture $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule (Figure 1b), endowing ZJU-74 with the potentially ultrahigh $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity and capture capacity.
The permanent porosity of the activated ZJU-74a was ­confirmed by nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ adsorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . As shown in Figure S3, Supporting Information, ZJU-74a takes up a $200.7~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ amount of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, and the nitrogen isotherm shows a significant type I sorption behavior without any hysteresis, characteristic of microporous materials. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface area was calculated to be $694~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The pore size distribution is shown in Figure S4, Supporting Information, determined by Horvath-Kawazoe method based on $77\mathrm{~K~N~}_{2}$ isotherms. The calculated pore size $(3.9\mathrm{~\AA~})$ is in good agreement with the value (3.6 Å) obtained from the crystal structure.
Pure component equilibrium adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were measured at different temperatures up to 1 bar (Figures S5–S8, Supporting Information), respectively. As shown in  Figure  2a, ZJU-74a shows extremely steep variable temperature adsorption isotherms at very low pressure, and almost saturation $(\sim76\ c m^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ at around 0.1 bar and 296 K. The total $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 296 K and 1 bar can reach $85.7\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . This $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 0.1 bar approaches the stoichiometric quantity (3.35 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ or $75~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ expected if one gas molecule is adsorbed per $\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}$ site, indicating that the $\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}$ sites mainly account for its low-pressure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. Due to the record-high density of OMSs and sandwich-like adsorption sites between two $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ units, ZJU-74a exhibits the ultra-strong affinity toward $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule, resulting in an exceptionally high gravimetric C2H2 uptake of 49.0 cm3 g−1 at 0.01 bar and $296\ \mathrm{K}.$ . As shown in Figure  2c and Table S1, ­Supporting Information, this $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake is higher than all the MOFs reported including the previous benchmark NKMOF$\boldsymbol{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}^{[20]}$ $(39\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{UTSA}–200\mathsf{a}^{[10\mathrm{c}]}$ $(41\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , setting a new benchmark for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture uptake at 0.01 bar. On the contrary, ZJU-74a shows significantly low $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes at low pressure regions (Figure 2b). These adsorption differences between these gases were also confirmed by the isosteric heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ , calculated by adsorption isotherms at different temperatures. As shown in Figure  2d, the $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are in the range of $45{-}65\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , which is notably higher than that of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the whole range. The maximum $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ value of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for ZJU-74a is even higher than those MOFs with high density of OMSs, such as NKMOF-1-Ni $(60.3\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1})}$ ,[20] Zn-MOF-74 $(31.7\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ,[19] and UTSA-74a $(33\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ,[19] further confirming its benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity and capture capacity. The time-dependent desorption kinetics studies of ZJU-74a showed that the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be completely removed under a high vacuum for ca. 36 and $16~\mathrm{min}$ at 296 K (Figure S14, Supporting Information), respectively.
The adsorption selectivities of ZJU-74a for $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ， $1/99\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ and $50/50\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures were predicted by using ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST), respectively. Figure 2e shows the data obtained at $296~\mathrm{K}$ . Due to the benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture capacity at low pressure, ZJU-74a exhibits an ultrahigh $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity up to 170 at 0.01 bar and $296\mathrm{~K~}$ With the increase of pressure, it gradually decreases down to 36.5 at 1 bar. It is worth to note that this $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity is the highest reported so far among all the robust MOFs at ambient conditions (Figure  2f and Figure S15, Supporting Information), notably higher than the previous bestperforming materials such as NKMOF-1-Ni (22),[20] HOF-3a (21),[22] JCM-1 (13.7),[15a] and UTSA-74a (9).[19] Similarly, ZJU-74a also exhibits an ultrahigh selectivity of over 15000 and 173.5 for $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $1/99\ C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm C_{2}\mathrm H_{4}$ mixtures at 0.01 bar and $296~\mathrm{K},$ respectively, which can be maintained to 1312.9 and 24.2 at 1 bar for these two mixtures. These benchmark gas selectivities render ZJU-74a among the best-performing materials reported for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture from some industrially relevant $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation applications.
To gain better insight into the benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture capacity and selectivity of ZJU-74a, grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations were performed to investigate the interactions between ZJU-74a and various gas molecules. The calculated binding sites of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are shown in Figure 3a,b. The $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules are preferentially located at the middle of two open nickel(II) sites within the square-shaped channels of ZJU-74a, displaying a side-on binding mode. The $\mathsf{C}\equiv\mathsf{C}$ bond of each $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule interacts with two $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions with a distance of $3.554\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , which are comparable with the sum of the vdW radii of carbon $(1.70\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and nickel $(1.63\mathrm{~\AA})$ atoms. This distance is even notably shorter than that found in Co-MOF-74 $_{(\approx4.0}$ Å),[17a] indicating a stronger interactions between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and metal ions in ZJU-74a. Additionally, two hydrogen atoms of each $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule are also bound by eight nitrogen atoms from two different $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions through cooperative $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsN}$ H-bonding with a distance of $4.173\mathring\mathrm{A}$ . Evidently, two $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ building units from adjacent layers can construct a sandwichlike binding environment to dually and strongly interact with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , thus affording its benchmark affinity and capture capacity for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The similar side-on binding mode was also observed on $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecule (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Unlike $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule is located at the middle of two open nickel(II) sites, but interact with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions through ${\mathrm{Ni}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{O}}={\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ with end-on configuration $({\approx}2.935\mathrm{~\AA})$ . This binding model without the sandwich-like multi-binding sites enables it to show a weaker interaction with the MOF. The calculated binding energy for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is $38\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ and $23\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively, which are consistent well with the higher experimental $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ than that of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 2d). In addition, we note that a full occupancy of these OMSs would correspond to 3.35 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ gas uptake, which is very close to the experimental $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake $(3.39\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1}})$ at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar. These calculation results are fully consistent with our experimental findings, which can explain the benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption and separation behaviors at low pressure qualitatively.
Transient breakthrough simulations were performed to validate the feasibility of using ZJU-74a in a fixed bed for the separation of $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $1/99~\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ and $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures, respectively. Figure  3c reveals the molar concentrations of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ or $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ exiting the adsorber packed with ZJU-74a as a function of the dimensionless time, $\tau,$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. Efficient separation can be accomplished by ZJU-74a for all the gas mixtures, whereby $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4},$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas breakthrough occurred first for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ or $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation, respectively, and then $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ passed through the fixed bed after a certain time $(\tau_{\mathrm{break}})$ because $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is more efficiently captured by the ­ZJU-74a bed.
Next, experimental breakthrough studies were performed in a packed column of activated ZJU-74a to evaluate its separation performance for actual $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (dry and wet), 1/99 $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ , and $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures at room temperature under a total flow of $2~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , respectively. As shown in Figure  3d, it was clear that ZJU-74a can effectively separate dry $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ gas mixture, wherein $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas first eluted through the adsorption bed, while $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ breakthrough did not occur until $68\ \mathrm{\min}$ . During this breakthrough process, the captured $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ amount for a given cycle was calculated to be 3.64 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $95\%$ of the saturated uptake $(3.83~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}})$ from the single-component gas isotherms at ambient conditions. According to the breakthrough curves, the selectivity of ZJU-74a for dry $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ mixture was calculated up to 4.3, higher than that of the benchmark NKMOF-1-Ni (2.6),[20] SNNU-45 (2.9),[13b] HOF-3a (2.0),[22] and FJU-22a (1.9).[15c] To investigate the influence of moisture, we conducted the breakthrough experiments on a wet $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ (50/50) mixture with $60\%$ humidity. As shown in Figure S18 and Table S5, Supporting Information, ZJU-74a can mostly retain its exceptional separation performance with the selectivity of 4.1 and $88\%$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity (dry), in which the small decrease may arise from small amount of OMSs occupied by water molecules. In addition, after exposure to an air environment with $50\mathrm{-}70\%$ humidity for 2 months, the re-activated material still possesses nearly the same separation performance for dry $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ mixture (Figure S19, Supporting Information). These results indicate that moisture only has a little effect on the separation capacity of ZJU-74a. After the breakthrough experiments, the material can be readily regenerated under a He flow $(10\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})$ at $373\ \mathrm{K}.$ . As depicted in Figure S20, Supporting Information, the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas can be completely recovered with high purity and no detectable $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ were found at the outlet after $26\mathrm{min}$ .
For 1/99 $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and 50/50 $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ mixtures (Figure 3e,f), ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ passed through the adsorption bed firstly, and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ breakthrough occurred at $140\mathrm{min}$ and $45\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Detailed gas chromatography data revealed that the concentration of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the outlet effluent is less than 5 ppm before breakthrough, affording high purity of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ up to $99.9995\%$ . During the breakthrough processes, the pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ production from the outlet effluent for a given cycle were analyzed to be 12.22 and $3.21\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. These breakthrough results reveal that ZJU-74a preferentially adsorbs $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in all the tested gas separations due to its benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture capacity, thus resulting in the exceptional performance for all these gas separations. Multiple $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ (50/50) mixedgas column breakthrough tests indicate that the separation performance of ZJU-74a can be recycled at least five continuous cycles (Figure S21, Supporting Information), confirming its good recyclability for gas separation. As inferred from the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and gas adsorption experiments on associated samples, ZJU-74a remains its structural integrality after multi­ple adsorption tests and various breakthrough experiments (Figures S22–S24, Supporting Information).
The $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -relevant separation tasks are typically implemented under more extreme conditions in real-world applications. For example, in the cases of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ raw streams, produced from the combustion of natural gas or the cracking of hydrocarbons, typically contain a small amount of water and acidic gases.[23] In the production of high-purity ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4},$ the feed gases for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ removal are also contaminated with trace levels of water.[24] These extreme separation conditions require adsorbents with extremely high and long-term water and $\mathrm{\pH}$ stabilities. We thus investigated the chemical stability of ZJU-74 toward water and acid-base environments. We exposed ZJU-74 samples to different chemical environments for 3 days, including water, boiling water, aqueous solutions of $\mathrm{\pH}$ between 1 and 12, 6 M HCl and $^{18\mathrm{~M~}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . As shown in Figure 4a, Figures S25 and S26, Supporting Information, the framework of ZJU-74 is stable in these conditions for several days, and no loss in crystallinity and no phase change were observed by PXRD. Moreover, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ after treatment are very close to those of as-synthesized materials, further confirming its ultra-highly chemical stability (Figure 4b and Figure S27, Supporting Information). Variable temperature PXRD patterns revealed that ZJU-74 shows an excellent thermal stability up to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without phase changes observed (Figure S28, Supporting Information). To be able to compare the materials, we also tested the chemical stability of some topperforming $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -selective MOFs and stable MOFs reported, including UTSA-74, Zn-MOF-74, TIFSIX-2-Cu-i, UiO-66, ZIF-8, and so on (Table S2, Supporting Information). On the basis of the PXRD analysis and literature report (Figures S29–S35, Supporting Information), ZJU-74a shows the best chemical stability among the indicated MOFs, even comparable to some of the state-of-the-art MOFs and COFs.[25] Furthermore, bulk samples of ZJU-74 are easily synthesized and scaled up from commercially available and inexpensive reagents by using a fast and direct mixing method at room temperature, as inferred from the PXRD (Figures S36 and S37, Supporting Information). Overall, by virtue of the specifically designed sandwich-like binding sites of a known, chemically-stable, ultramicroporous MOF made by common and inexpensive reagents, highly efficient capture of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from various other gases has been realized for promising energy-efficient separation technologies.
In summary, we reported that a chemically stable Hofmanntype MOF (ZJU-74a), featuring record-high density of OMSs and unique sandwich-like binding sites, exhibits a new benchmark for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture at low pressure. This maximizes the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -related separation performance, affording the highest $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity for robust MOFs reported so for and very high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivities. We attribute this ultrastrong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture capacity to the unique sandwich-like binding environments, in which the oppositely adjacent open metal centers and cyanide groups from adjacent $\mathrm{[Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ units can dually and synergistically interact with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule and thus provide the very strong binding affinity for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , as indicated by theoretical calculations. Simulated and breakthrough experiments demonstrated that ZJU-74a can efficiently capture and separate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ (dry and wet), $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ mixtures, respectively. Combined with its extraordinary water $/\mathrm{pH}$ stability and easy synthesis from common and inexpensive reagents, ZJU-74 represents a new benchmark material that has the potential to be applied for industrial challenges related to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation. Our work not only reports the benchmark porous material for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture and separation, but also provides a new approach to the development of porous materials with strong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding affinity to address some relevant and challenging gas separations.
(IGA001, Hiden, UK) under diverse operating conditions. To remove all the guest solvents in the framework, the fresh powder samples were first solvent-exchanged with dry acetone at least 10 times within two days, and evacuated at room temperature (296 K) for $24\ h$ and further at $363~\mathsf{K}$ for $\rceil2\mathrm{~h~}$ until the outgas rate was $5\mathsf{m m H g}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ prior to measurements. The sorption measurement was maintained at $77~\mathsf{K}$ under liquid nitrogen bath. Julabo water bath was used to keep the adsorption tube at a constant temperature of 273, 288, 296, 313, and $333\mathrm{~K},$ respectively.
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction data of ZJU-74 were collected on an Agilent Supernova CCD diffractometer equipped with graphitemonochromatic enhanced Mo-Ka radiation $(\lambda=0.77073\mathrm{\AA})$ at $\begin{array}{r}{{100\ \mathsf{K}}.}\end{array}$ The datasets were corrected by empirical absorption correction using spherical harmonics, implemented in the SCALE3 ABSPACK scaling algorithm. The structure was solved by direct methods and refined by full matrix least-squares methods with the SHELX-97 program package.[26] The pyridine ligand is disordered. The crystal data are summarized in Table S3, Supporting Information.
The breakthrough experiments were performed in dynamic gas breakthrough equipment using a stainless steel column $(4.0\ \mathsf{m m}$ inner diameter $\times120~\mathsf{m m}$ ). The weight of sample packed in the column was: $0.8329\mathrm{g}$ . The mixed gas flows of (1) dry and wet $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}=50/50\ (\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ , (2) $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}=7/99\ (v/v)$ , (3) $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{2}/\mathsf C\mathsf H_{4}=50/50\mathrm\mathrm{~(v~}$ $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ , and (4) $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/$ $\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{6}=50/50\mathrm\mathsf\Lambda(\mathsf v/\mathsf v)$ were then introduced at $2\ m|\ m i\ n^{-1}$ . The desorption test of ZJU-74a was conducted under a sweeping He gas with a flow of $10\ m L\ m i n^{-1}$ at $373\ K.$ Outlet gas from the column was monitored using gas chromatography (GC-2014C, SHIMADZU) with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD, detection limit $0.1~\mathsf{p p m})$ . The standard gases were used to calibrate the concentration of the outlet gas. After the breakthrough tests, ZJU-74a can be readily regenerated by sweeping He gas $(70m L\min^{-1})$ at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ .
